From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a console draws the pointer it is tracking

With patch 0025 the desktop on a tty took the keyboard and answered
chords. It also logged `the first pointer event reached the window`
before it logged the first key -- the trackpad works and always did --
and drew no pointer at all.

THE ARROW IS IN THE BINARY. `ui_lottie_resources.grd:10` gates the
cursor block on `use_aura`, not on ChromeOS; `chrome/chrome_paks.gni:152`
puts that pak in `chrome_extra_paks` unconditionally; and
`skia_support_skottie` is `!is_android`, so the rasteriser is here too.
Nothing asks for it.

`wm::CursorLoader::CursorFromType` asks the platform's factory first and
returns any non-null answer (`ui/wm/core/cursor_loader.cc:194-203`), so
`LoadCursorFromAsset` on the next line runs only when the platform
answers nothing. `BitmapCursorFactory` never answers nothing. It hands
back a `BitmapCursor` carrying the TYPE and an empty bitmap vector, and
its own comment says why:

    // Return a cursor not backed by a bitmap to preserve the type
    // information. It can still be used to request the compositor to
    // draw a server-side cursor for the given type.

Which is exactly right for a platform whose compositor draws a cursor
from a name, and exactly wrong for one that hands a CRTC a buffer. The
empty vector goes to `DrmCursor::SendCursorShowLocked`
(`drm_cursor.cc:273-281`), to `HardwareDisplayController::SetCursor`
with a bitmap that `drawsNothing()` (`:427-436`), to `UpdateCursorImage`
leaving `handle = 0` (`:698-711`), to
`drmModeSetCursor(fd, crtc, 0, 0, 0)`. Handle zero is the kernel's word
for TURN THE CURSOR OFF. The ioctl succeeds, so the `PLOG(ERROR)` at
`crtc_controller.cc:86` never fires and the whole thing is silent.

ASH HAS THE SAME FACTORY AND NOT THE SAME BUG, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS
ONE DEFAULT ARGUMENT. `ash::NativeCursorManagerAsh` holds
`wm::CursorLoader cursor_loader_{/*use_platform_cursors=*/false}`
(`native_cursor_manager_ash.h:64`), so on ChromeOS the factory is never
asked and the assets are always loaded.
`views::DesktopNativeCursorManager` holds a plain `wm::CursorLoader`
(`desktop_native_cursor_manager.h:73`) and that parameter defaults to
true (`cursor_loader.h:31`). ozone/drm's only upstream consumer is ash.
This is the fifth thing in this series that ash supplies and a views
browser does not, after `Close()`, `Activate()`, DRM master and
`DisplayConfigurator::TakeControl`.

So the DRM platform gets a factory that answers nothing, and the loader
reaches the arrow.

`kNone` IS THE ONE THAT MUST STILL BE ANSWERED. `CursorLoader` routes it
through the platform whatever `use_platform_cursors_` says, because an
invisible cursor is made differently on every platform -- and here
`SendCursorShowLocked` reads `type() == kNone` as "hide", which needs
the typed, bitmapless object the base class makes. Answering nothing for
it would send the loader to assets that have no art for an invisible
cursor and fall back to the pointer: an arrow drawn exactly where
something asked for no cursor at all.

NOT A GLOBAL FLIP OF `use_platform_cursors`, WHICH WOULD BE THE OBVIOUS
ONE-LINER. `build.sh` builds one binary carrying
`ozone_platform_wayland`, `ozone_platform_headless` and
`ozone_platform_drm`, and Wayland's factory supplies real theme bitmaps
-- it overrides both `GetDefaultCursor` arities. Turning the parameter
off in `DesktopNativeCursorManager` would take the system cursor theme
away from every nested run to fix the one platform that has no theme.

`cursor_loader.cc:201` now logs `Failed to load a platform cursor of
type N` once per type. That is the loader narrating the fallback this
exists to reach, and it is not a failure.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. Every link above is read at the pin, and the
two ends are independently pinned by upstream's own unit test
(`bitmap_cursor_factory_unittest.cc:26-44` asserts the bitmapless
answer) and by ash's `false`. What has never been executed by this
project on any machine is `drmModeSetCursor` with a real buffer --
`crux` has no panel. If a pointer still does not draw after this, the
cursor plane is the next thing to look at and
`crtc_controller.cc:86`'s `PLOG` is the line that will say so.

